language ESSENCE' 1.0
$ session2_color_simple: an Essence' model from DCP Rosetta.
$
$ Assign a colour to each of six countries (Belgium, Denmark, France, Germany, Netherlands and Luxembourg) so that two neighbouring countries never share a colour. Colours are the integers 1, 2, 3, and so on. Find a colouring that minimises the number of colours used.
$
$ Save this file as model.eprime. Download `session2_color_simple.json` and `dcp_convert_essence.py` from the
$ problem's page:
$   https://dcp-bench.github.io/DCP-Rosetta/problems/session2_color_simple.html
$ Convert the instances once, then run the first one with Savile Row 1.11 (it needs Java); the
$ solution is written to `session2_color_simple_1.param.solution`:
$   python dcp_convert_essence.py session2_color_simple.json
$   savilerow model.eprime session2_color_simple_1.param -run-solver

$ number of countries, numbered 1 to n in the order Belgium, Denmark, France, Germany, Netherlands, Luxembourg
letting n = 6

$ graph_dim1: number of neighbouring pairs (derived by the converter from the data; not a field of the reader's JSON)
given graph_dim1 : int
$ graph[e,1] and graph[e,2]: the two countries of neighbouring pair e (1-based country numbers)
given graph : matrix indexed by [int(1..graph_dim1), int(1..2)] of int(1..n)

$ colors[c]: colour of country c, a colour being an integer from 1 to n
find colors : matrix indexed by [int(1..n)] of int(1..n)
$ num_colours: auxiliary, the number of colours used, which is the largest colour assigned
find num_colours : int(1..n)

minimising num_colours

such that
$ the number of colours used is the largest colour assigned to a country
num_colours = max(colors),
$ every pair of neighbouring countries must receive different colours
forAll e : int(1..graph_dim1) . colors[graph[e,1]] != colors[graph[e,2]]
